/*
 * AG LAB · long report copy, part 2: Day Master, element balance, strength, signs, resilience,
 * and the deeper chart reading for the email.
 */
(function (root) {
  "use strict";
  var L = root.LAB || (root.LAB = {});

  // ---- shown on the page ----

  // Index = heavenly stem: 0 甲 … 9 癸. You as a maker, through the stem’s image.
  L.dayMasterDeep = [
    { zh: "甲木日主，像一棵参天大树：认准一个方向，就一直往上长。在工作室里，这常常表现为一个你反复追问的问题，从一个项目带到下一个项目，靠一层层积累长高，而不是突然跳跃。小组合作时，你往往是搭起整体结构的人；第一次 crit 之后，你也可能是最不愿放弃速写本里那个想法的人。你的长处是有骨架、有担当，需要练习的是适度弯曲。修剪能让树长得更好，所以不妨把删改看成生长而非损失：让导师的意见为你的力量换一个方向，而不是动摇你的根。给自己时间，你最好的作品往往按季节到来，而不是按天。",
      en: "As a Jia Wood (甲木) Day Master, you work like a tall tree: you choose a direction and keep growing towards it. In the studio this often looks like a single, stubborn question you carry from project to project, gaining height through accumulated layers rather than sudden leaps. You may be the person who sets up the structure of a group piece, or who refuses to abandon a sketchbook idea after the first crit. Your strength is backbone; your challenge is bending. Pruning helps a tree, so treat editing as growth, not loss. Let a tutor’s cut redirect your energy rather than threaten your roots, and give yourself time: your best work tends to arrive in seasons, not days." },
    { zh: "乙木日主，像藤萝与花草：柔韧、灵巧，总能找到光，也善于顺着身边的结构生长。在实践中，你常常能从一份命题、一件拾得物或同伴的一个想法出发，把自己的感受一圈圈缠绕进去，直到结果明显带着你的气质。别人跨过去的小缝隙，你会注意到；你懂得调整，却不会失去自己的形状。需要留意的是同时向四面八方蔓延，或者在某个支撑上靠得太久。每个项目选一个“架子”：一条清晰的限制，或一种单一的材料，让你的灵活在其中发挥到最好。温和而持续的坚持，正是你真正的力量。",
      en: "As a Yi Wood (乙木) Day Master, you work like a vine or a meadow of grasses: supple, quick to find the light, and clever at growing along whatever structure is nearby. In practice you may thrive on a brief, a found object or a collaborator’s idea, winding your own sensibility through it until the result is unmistakably yours. You notice small openings that others step over, and you adapt without losing your shape. The risk is spreading in every direction at once, or leaning on one support for too long. Choose a single trellis per project, a clear constraint or one material, and let your flexibility do its best work inside it. Gentle persistence is your real strength." },
    { zh: "丙火日主，像太阳：你自带光热，一走进房间，气氛就会改变。在小组 crit 里，你常常是带动能量的人，能让停滞的项目重新热起来，也能一眼看出作品需要的那个大动作。色彩、尺度和面向公众的作品，可能对你来说很自然；你先迈出一步，别人往往也就敢了。不过太阳照耀万物，注意力也容易分散：你可能热情地开启很多事，却完成得较少。不妨为自己设定每天的“正午”：一段固定的时间，把所有的光都集中在一件作品上。休息同样重要。太阳也会落山，傍晚的沉淀，才让第二天的明亮更真诚。",
      en: "As a Bing Fire (丙火) Day Master, you work like the sun: you radiate, and the room changes when you arrive. You often bring energy to a group crit, warm up a stalled project, or see the big gesture a piece needs. Colour, scale and public-facing work may come naturally, and you can make other people braver simply by going first. The sun shines on everything, though, so focus can scatter; you may start many things with heat and finish fewer. Try setting a daily “noon”: one fixed block of time when all your light falls on a single piece. Rest matters too. Even the sun sets, and your evenings of reflection keep tomorrow’s brightness honest." },
    { zh: "丁火日主，像灯烛：一团稳定而贴近的火光，能照见强光下被冲淡的东西。你可能偏爱细节、亲密感和慢慢的凝视：页边的批注、台灯下的小物件、crit 结束后的一对一谈话。别人看你的作品时，常常有被看见的感觉。烛火需要遮蔽，所以嘈杂拥挤的工作室可能比你预想的更消耗你；给自己留一个安静的角落，让火苗保持平稳。需要留意的是为了一个小小的部分熬到深夜。给自己定一个温和的收工时间，随手记下灵感的火星，并相信：一处被好好照亮的细节，足以撑起整个空间。",
      en: "As a Ding Fire (丁火) Day Master, you work like candlelight: a steady, close flame that reveals what brighter lights wash out. You may be drawn to detail, intimacy and slow attention: the annotation in the margin, the small object under a lamp, the one-to-one conversation after a crit. People often feel seen by your work. A candle needs shelter, so noisy, crowded studios can tire you faster than you expect; protect a corner where your flame can stay still. The risk is burning late into the night over one tiny element. Set a gentle finishing time, keep a notebook of sparks, and trust that a single well-lit detail can hold a whole room." },
    { zh: "戊土日主，像一座高山：稳重、宽厚，不容易被撼动。在工作室里，你可能是别人愿意依靠的人：小组项目摇摆时，是你守住了计划；你的作品也常常带着沉静而有分量的体量感。你习惯从结构、体块和长周期去思考，能不慌不忙地扛起一个大而慢的项目。需要留意的是“静”太久：你可能迟迟不肯开始，或者只因为改变像是被侵蚀，就本能地抗拒反馈。不妨把小小的实验看成天气，而不是地震。高山正是被无数场细雨塑造的，每次你允许作品松动一点，它都会变得更丰富。",
      en: "As a Wu Earth (戊土) Day Master, you work like a mountain: steady, broad and hard to move. In the studio you may be the one others lean on, the person who holds the plan when a group project wobbles, or whose work has a calm, monumental weight. You often think in structure, mass and long timelines, and you can carry a large, slow project without panic. The shadow side is stillness: you may wait too long to begin, or resist feedback simply because change feels like erosion. Treat small experiments as weather rather than earthquakes. A mountain is shaped by many light rains, and your work grows richer each time you let a little of it shift." },
    { zh: "己土日主，像被细心照料的田园：包容、耐心，默默地生出收成。你最擅长的往往是“耕耘”：把一个粗糙的想法翻土、浇水、除草，直到它真正长出东西。对你来说，过程常常和结果一样重要；你的速写本，可能比你自己承认的还要丰富。你也会照料身边的人，注意到工作室里谁的版画或截止日期需要帮手。需要留意的是吸收得太多，包括别人的意见，以至于找不到自己的那颗种子。给自己留一小块只属于你的地：一个项目，或者每天一小时，在那里你是唯一的园丁。",
      en: "As a Ji Earth (己土) Day Master, you work like a tended field or garden: receptive, patient and quietly productive. You may be at your best cultivating, taking a rough idea and turning it, watering it and weeding it until something real grows. Process often matters to you as much as outcome; your sketchbooks may be richer than you admit. You also tend other people, noticing who in the studio needs a hand with a print or a deadline. The risk is absorbing too much, other people’s opinions included, until your own seed is hard to find. Keep a small plot that is only yours: one project, or one hour a day, where you are the only gardener." },
    { zh: "庚金日主，像矿石，也像由它锻成的刀剑：刚强、果断、讲原则。你往往能迅速切中一个项目真正要说的东西，无论在创作还是在 crit 里，都不太能容忍含糊。坚硬的材料、清晰的系统和大刀阔斧的删改，可能都很适合你；如果方向不对，你敢于推翻三个星期的工作。矿石要经过火炼才能成刃，所以适度的压力和挑战，反而能让你更锋利。需要留意的是下刀太早，一个想法还没来得及展开就被你砍掉了。删改之前，不妨先停一天。锋利加上耐心，才会变成真正的手艺。",
      en: "As a Geng Metal (庚金) Day Master, you work like raw ore and the blade forged from it: strong, decisive and principled. You may cut quickly to what a project is really about, and you often have little patience for vagueness, in your work or in a crit. Hard materials, clear systems and bold edits can suit you; you are not afraid to throw out three weeks of work if the direction is wrong. Ore becomes a blade only through fire, so a fair amount of pressure and challenge can sharpen you. The risk is cutting too soon, before an idea has had time to reveal itself. Before a big edit, pause for one day. Sharpness with patience becomes real craft." },
    { zh: "辛金日主，像珠玉与精金：精致、敏锐，对表面与完成度格外在意。你可能会注意到纸张的克重、阴影里那一点准确的灰、一条线偏了的四分之一毫米。你的作品往往经得起细看，你的品味也常常默默成为整个小组的指南针。珠玉要经过打磨、镶嵌才会发光，所以好的呈现对你来说确实重要。需要留意的是完美主义：因为不够无瑕就迟迟不肯拿出作品，或者把 crit 听得比对方本意更尖锐。不妨偶尔在“原石”阶段就分享作品。打磨是你的天赋，而不是被看见的前提。",
      en: "As a Xin Metal (辛金) Day Master, you work like jewels and fine metal: refined, perceptive and acutely aware of surface and finish. You may notice the weight of a paper stock, the exact grey of a shadow, the quarter-millimetre by which a line is off. Your work often rewards close looking, and your taste can be a quiet compass for a whole group. Jewels shine once they are polished and set, so good presentation genuinely matters to you. The risk is perfectionism: holding a piece back because it is not flawless, or hearing a crit as sharper than it was meant. Try sharing work at the “rough gem” stage now and then. Polish is your gift, not a condition for being seen." },
    { zh: "壬水日主，像奔流的江河：开阔、不安分，始终在流动。你可能被宏大的观念、跨学科的研究、旅行、档案，以及一切能把遥远事物连起来的东西吸引。你的项目常常从很宽处开始，而且越走越宽，把哲学、电影和街头的素材汇进同一股水流。这种视野是真正的天赋，你能看见别人错过的联系。不过没有河岸的河会泛滥，所以结构对你很有帮助：一个清晰的问题、一个截止日期、一个早早定下的最终形式。让这些河岸成为方向，而不是束缚。有了河道，你的能量会流得更远，也更有力量。",
      en: "As a Ren Water (壬水) Day Master, you work like a great river: expansive, restless and always on the move. You may be drawn to big ideas, research that crosses disciplines, travel, archives and anything that connects distant places. Your projects often begin wide and keep widening, carrying references from philosophy, film and the street into one current. That reach is a real gift; you see links others miss. A river without banks floods, though, so structure helps: a clear question, a deadline, a final format chosen early. Let those banks feel like direction rather than confinement. Your energy flows further, and with more force, once it has a channel to run through." },
    { zh: "癸水日主，像雨露：细腻、无声地渗透，悄悄改变一切。你可能更擅长通过氛围、暗示和想象来创作，而不是直接陈述；你的想法常常以半成形的样子到来，来自梦、无意听到的一句话，或者光线的一次变化。你的影响是渐进的，参加 crit 的人可能事后才发现你的作品一直留在心里。你对情绪的感受往往很深，所以热闹的工作室容易让你疲惫。需要留意的是太过柔和，让作品始终落不了地。给每个项目一个坚定的锚点：一个标题、一种材料或一个尺度，让雾气围绕它聚拢。一点一滴耐心收集起来，就是一整片风景。",
      en: "As a Gui Water (癸水) Day Master, you work like rain and dew: subtle, seeping and quietly transformative. You may work through atmosphere, suggestion and imagination rather than statement, and your ideas often arrive half-formed, from dreams, overheard phrases or a change in the light. Your influence is gradual; people at a crit may realise only later how much your piece stayed with them. You are often deeply receptive to mood, which can make busy studios tiring. The risk is staying so soft that the work never quite lands. Give each project one firm anchor, a title, a material or a scale, and let the mist gather around it. Small drops, collected patiently, become a whole landscape." }
  ];

  // What this Day Master strength means for working style.
  L.strengthDeep = {
    "extreme-strong": { zh: "日主极强、呈专旺之势时，你往往带着很大的惯性和鲜明的个人印记工作。比起被命题牵着走，你可能更想主导命题；你的风格也许隔着整个房间就能认出来。不妨顺着这股势头：投入系列作品、长周期项目和属于自己的语言。只是记得给意外留一扇门，比如一位合作者或一种陌生的材料，让你的力量保持流动，而不是变得僵硬。",
      en: "A very strong Day Master (专旺) often works with great momentum and a clear inner signature. You may prefer to lead a brief rather than follow it, and your style can be recognisable from across the room. Go with that current: commit to series, long projects and a personal language. Just leave a door open for surprise, a collaborator or an unfamiliar material, so your strength keeps moving rather than hardening." },
    "strong": { zh: "身强的日主，通常精力有余。你往往能扛住长时间的工作，在 crit 中坚持自己的想法，也能较快地从挫折中恢复。真正值得思考的，是这些多出来的能量去了哪里。把它引向外部：多做、多展出、把一项技法教给同伴，或接下一份有难度的命题。能量有了出口，就会变成作品；没有出口，就可能变成焦躁或固执。",
      en: "A strong Day Master (身强) usually has energy to spare. You can often push through long sessions, defend an idea in a crit and recover quickly from setbacks. The useful question is where that surplus goes. Channel it outwards: into making more, exhibiting, teaching a technique to a peer or taking on a demanding brief. When energy has an outlet, it becomes work; without one, it may turn into restlessness or stubbornness." },
    "slightly-strong": { zh: "日主偏强时，你往往自信而不僵硬：既能守住自己的方向，也听得进好的建议。最适合你的节奏，可能是稳定地产出，同时定期接受坦诚的反馈：挑战足以让你保持敏锐，独立又足以让作品仍然属于你。需要留意的是，自信偶尔会让你跳过研究这一步；多听一点、多查一点，常常能让作品再上一个台阶。",
      en: "A slightly strong Day Master (偏强) often feels self-assured without being rigid. You can usually hold your own direction and still take in a good suggestion. Your best rhythm may be steady output with regular, honest feedback: enough challenge to keep you sharp, enough independence to keep the work yours. Watch for moments when confidence slides into skipping research; a little extra listening often lifts the piece further." },
    "slightly-weak": { zh: "日主偏弱时，你往往敏锐而善于回应，能察觉语境、材料和身边人的需要。身边有一些支持时，你可能工作得最好：一份清晰的命题、一位信任的同伴、一套把精力留给创作本身的日常流程。慢慢搭建一些小小的根基，比如一个参考资料库或一套可靠的工作方法，会让你感到稳定许多。早一点征求意见，而不是独自扛下每一个决定。",
      en: "A slightly weak Day Master (偏弱) is often perceptive and responsive, picking up on context, materials and other people’s needs. You may work best with some support around you: a clear brief, a trusted peer, a routine that saves your energy for the making itself. Building small foundations, such as a reference library or a reliable process, can make you feel much steadier. Ask for input early rather than carrying every decision alone." },
    "weak": { zh: "身弱的日主，往往有一份调得很细的敏感：你对氛围、批评和可能性的感受，比大多数人更鲜明。这能让你的作品细腻而富有同理心，也可能让忙碌的工作周格外消耗你。有意识地保护自己的精力：同时进行的项目少一些，规律地休息，选择给出具体而温和反馈的导师。用八字的话说，印星与比劫是你的助力，所以多从书本中汲取养分，也多依靠你的同伴。",
      en: "A weak Day Master (身弱) often comes with a finely tuned sensitivity: you register atmosphere, criticism and possibility more vividly than most. That can make your work subtle and empathetic, and it can also make busy studio weeks draining. Protect your energy deliberately: fewer projects at once, regular rest, mentors who give specific, kind feedback. In Bazi terms, Resource and Peers are your allies, so learn from books and lean on your community." },
    "extreme-weak": { zh: "日主极弱、呈从势之象，是一种特殊格局：命局不是靠日主独立支撑，而是顺从最旺的那股力量。在创作上，你可能特别擅长进入一个语境，比如一个场地、一个社群或一份命题，并让它来塑造作品。这种回应能力是真正的天赋，而不是欠缺。好好选择你身处的环境，因为它会滋养你；也请轻松看待这一解读，它值得导师再仔细看一看。",
      en: "A very weak Day Master (从势) is a special pattern: rather than standing alone, the chart flows with whichever force is strongest. Creatively, you may be remarkably good at entering a context, a site, a community or a brief, and letting it shape the work. That responsiveness is a genuine talent, not a lack. Choose your surroundings carefully, because they feed you, and hold this reading lightly; it is worth a tutor’s second look." }
  };

  // Index = element (0 Wood … 4 Water): most prominent; absent; present only in hidden stems.
  L.elementBalance = [
    { strong: { zh: "命局中木最突出时，生长与开端可能对你来说很自然。你常常点子不断、速写很快，也能想象一个项目在几个月里如何发展。木气利于绘画、向外分枝的研究，以及关于生命与系统的作品。需要留意的是收尾：木总爱冒出新芽。给自己一个简单的截止日期和一次最后的删改，让这些生长真正结出果实。",
        en: "With Wood as your most prominent element, growth and beginnings may come easily. You often generate ideas, sketch quickly and imagine how a project could develop over months. Wood favours drawing, research that branches outwards and work about living systems. Keep an eye on finishing: Wood loves to put out new shoots. A simple end date and a final edit help all that growth bear fruit." },
      missing: { zh: "命局中缺木，可能只是意味着开端与长远规划需要你多花一点心思。比起从零发明，你也许更喜欢打磨已有的东西。可以温和地把木引进来：去树林里走走，维护一块不断生长的灵感板，每天不带评判地画几笔，多用纸、竹或木材。缺少的五行不是缺陷，而是值得好奇的方向。",
        en: "When Wood is absent from your chart, it may simply mean that beginnings and long-range planning take a little more conscious effort. You may prefer refining what exists to inventing from nothing. Bring Wood in gently: walk among trees, keep a growing mood board, sketch daily without judging, work with paper, bamboo or timber. Missing elements are not flaws; they are directions for curiosity." },
      hidden: { zh: "木只藏于地支藏干之中时，它的生长发生在地表之下。你可能有很少说出口的抱负和长远计划，或者一些想法会先悄悄扎根，之后才显露出来。给这份藏着的木一点阳光吧：写下你希望项目走向哪里，分享一张早期草图，或者在工作室的墙上贴一张看得见的时间线。",
        en: "When Wood appears only in the hidden stems (藏干), its growth happens below the surface. You may have ambitions and long-term plans you rarely say aloud, or ideas that take root quietly before they show. Give that hidden Wood a little daylight: write down where you want a project to go, share an early sketch, or keep a visible timeline on your studio wall." } },
    { strong: { zh: "命局中火最突出时，表达、热情与被看见可能是你的天性。你常常为作品带来色彩、情感和存在感，也可能享受展示、表演或讲述。火势蔓延得快，能量也容易忽高忽低。用一些更“凉”的阶段来调节节奏：研究、休息、安静地动手做。这样你的光亮就能贯穿整个项目，而不只在开头闪耀。",
        en: "With Fire as your most prominent element, expression, warmth and visibility may come naturally. You often bring colour, emotion and presence to your work, and you may enjoy showing, performing or presenting. Fire spreads quickly, so energy can peak and fade. Pace yourself with cooler phases: research, rest, quiet making. That way your brightness lasts through a whole project, not just its launch." },
      missing: { zh: "命局中缺火，你可能天生对展示作品、谈论作品比较含蓄。这种克制能让你的创作有深度、有沉静感。如果当众讲述让你为难，可以一小步一小步地把火引进来：练习一段两分钟的陈述，早一点把作品钉上墙，用一抹温暖的点缀色，或者在明亮、有阳光的空间里工作。被看见是可以慢慢练习的。",
        en: "When Fire is absent from your chart, you may be naturally reserved about showing your work or speaking about it. That restraint can give your practice depth and calm. If presenting feels hard, bring Fire in by small steps: rehearse a two-minute talk, pin work up early, use one warm accent colour, or work in a bright, sunlit space. Being seen is something you can practise, gently." },
      hidden: { zh: "火只藏于藏干之中时，你的热情可能比外表看起来更炽热。别人也许觉得你冷静、克制，而作品底下其实有很多情感在推动。让一部分热度浮上来：给作品起一个诚实的标题，讲讲它为什么对你重要，或者在作品集里留出一个片刻，允许情感大声一点。",
        en: "When Fire appears only in the hidden stems, your passion may run warmer than you show. People might read you as cool or composed while a great deal of feeling drives the work underneath. Let some of that heat surface: give a piece an honest title, talk about why it matters to you, or choose one moment in a portfolio where emotion is allowed to be loud." } },
    { strong: { zh: "命局中土最突出时，稳定、耐心和对过程的重视，可能就是你的根基。你常常细致地搭建作品，思考东西是怎么做出来的，截止日期临近时也能保持镇定。土气适合陶艺、雕塑、档案整理，以及一切有分量感的创作。需要留意的是沉滞：如果项目停住了，就引入一点流动，比如散一次步、画一张快速草图，或者找一位新的合作者。",
        en: "With Earth as your most prominent element, stability, patience and care for process may be your foundation. You often build work carefully, think about how things are made and stay calm as deadlines close in. Earth suits ceramics, sculpture, archives and anything with weight. Watch for heaviness: if a project stalls, introduce some movement, such as a walk, a quick sketch or a new collaborator." },
      missing: { zh: "命局中缺土，你的想法可能比落地的速度更快。没有清晰的根据地时，你也许会在几个方向之间跳来跳去，或者感到不太安定。可以通过日常和手感把土引进来：固定的工作时段，黏土、石膏或厚纸，一张整洁的桌子，每个项目一个专属的资料盒。有了根基，你的想法才有落脚的地方。",
        en: "When Earth is absent from your chart, ideas may move faster than your ability to ground them. You might jump between directions or feel unsettled without a clear base. Bring Earth in through routine and touch: a fixed working hour, clay, plaster or heavy paper, a tidy desk, a single archive box for each project. Grounding gives your ideas somewhere to land." },
      hidden: { zh: "土只藏于藏干之中时，你的稳重可能是一份安静的储备，而不是外显的特质。你也许会在危急时刻意外地保持冷静，或者默默把一个小组维系在一起。有意识地调用它吧：建立一个简单的每周结构，记录你的创作过程，并留意你其实已经撑起了多少事。这片藏着的土地，比看上去更坚实。",
        en: "When Earth appears only in the hidden stems, your steadiness may be a quiet reserve rather than an obvious trait. You may surprise yourself by staying calm in a crisis or quietly holding a group together. Draw on it consciously: build a simple weekly structure, document your process, and notice how much you already sustain. That hidden ground is firmer than it looks." } },
    { strong: { zh: "命局中金最突出时，清晰、精确和判断力可能主导着你的创作。你常常知道该删掉什么，也可能被利落的边缘、字体排印、金属工艺、摄影或严密的系统吸引。金对自己往往也很严格，所以一次 crit 对你来说，可能比对别人更尖锐。用玩心平衡精确：专门安排一些凌乱的实验，在那里什么都不必“对”。",
        en: "With Metal as your most prominent element, clarity, precision and judgement may lead your practice. You often know what to remove, and you may be drawn to clean edges, typography, metalwork, photography or tight systems. Metal can be strict with itself, so a crit may feel sharper to you than to others. Balance precision with play: schedule messy experiments where nothing has to be right." },
      missing: { zh: "命局中缺金，删改和决定对你来说，可能比产生想法更费力。因为舍不得下刀，你也许会让每一个想法都继续活着。可以用温和的结构把金引进来：一张清单，一份清晰的命题，一个严格的形式限制，比如只用一种尺寸或一组色彩，或者请一位信任的同伴帮你做选择。干脆利落地剪掉，往往会让人意外地轻松。",
        en: "When Metal is absent from your chart, editing and deciding may take more effort than generating. You might keep every idea alive because cutting feels hard. Bring Metal in with gentle structure: a checklist, a clear brief, a strict format such as a single size or palette, or a trusted peer who helps you choose. A clean cut can feel surprisingly freeing." },
      hidden: { zh: "金只藏于藏干之中时，你的批判眼光可能在后台默默运作。你也许在说清原因之前，就已经感觉到哪里不对。相信这份直觉，并给它找到语言：在 crit 时做笔记，为每一次删改写一句简短的理由，练习说出你会剪掉什么。这份藏着的判断力，一旦被说出来，就会变得真正有用。",
        en: "When Metal appears only in the hidden stems, your critical eye may work quietly in the background. You might sense that something is wrong before you can say why. Trust that instinct and give it words: take notes during crits, write a short reason for each edit, and practise naming what you would cut. Your hidden discernment becomes truly useful once it is spoken." } },
    { strong: { zh: "命局中水最突出时，研究、思考与想象可能都很深。你常常先想后做，广泛收集参考，留意记忆、地点与观念之间的联系。水气适合写作、影像、装置和以研究为主导的项目。不过水也容易漫流，所以给它一个容器吧：一个固定的形式、一个截止日期，或者每周都回到的同一个问题。",
        en: "With Water as your most prominent element, research, reflection and imagination may run deep. You often think before you make, collect references widely and notice connections between memory, place and idea. Water suits writing, moving image, installation and research-led projects. It can also drift, so give the flow a container: a fixed format, a deadline, or one question you return to each week." },
      missing: { zh: "命局中缺水，比起反思，你可能更喜欢动手，研究有时会像在绕远路。这种偏向行动的倾向很有用，也可以被平衡。用一些小习惯把水引进来：每周一小时的阅读，床头放一本笔记本，去图书馆、档案馆或河边走走，并在每个项目结束后留出时间，问问自己学到了什么。",
        en: "When Water is absent from your chart, you may prefer doing to reflecting, and research can feel like a detour. That bias towards action is useful, and it can be balanced. Bring Water in with small habits: a reading hour each week, a notebook by the bed, a visit to a library, an archive or a riverside path, and time after each project to ask what you learned." },
      hidden: { zh: "水只藏于藏干之中时，你的深度可能是私密的。你也许有丰富的内在参照、梦境和记忆，却很少进入作品。让这条地下的溪流浮出一些：写一本研究日志，为某个项目附上一段简短的文字思考，或者直接以一段记忆为基础做一件作品。藏着的水，往往滋养着最深的根。",
        en: "When Water appears only in the hidden stems, your depth may be private. You might have rich inner references, dreams and memories that rarely make it into the work. Let some of that underground stream surface: keep a research journal, add a short written reflection to a project, or base one piece directly on a memory. Hidden water often feeds the deepest roots." } }
  ];

  // Aries … Pisces: Sun (core drive), Moon (what you need), Rising (how you come across).
  L.zodiacDeep = [
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在白羊座，你的创作身份和“主动”紧紧相连。你常常在事情的开端最有活力：第一张草图、全新的命题、没人试过的实验。对你来说，速度可以是一种方法，而不是缺点。真正的功夫在于“回来”：火花落下之后，隔一天再回来，在它的基础上继续搭建，让最初的能量变成完成的作品。",
        en: "With the Sun in Aries, your creative identity is tied to initiative. You often thrive at the start of things: the first sketch, the fresh brief, the experiment no one has tried. Speed can be a method for you, not a flaw. The craft lies in returning: once the spark is down, come back a day later and build on it, so that first energy becomes finished work." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在白羊座，当事情在发生时，你可能最安心。短的截止期、友好的竞争和需要动手的制作，都能提振你的心情；漫长的等待或含糊的反馈，则可能让你烦躁。在一周里安排一些爆发时段：限时速写、快速做原型、crit 前快走一圈。温和地释放能量，能帮助你对自己更宽容一些。",
        en: "With the Moon in Aries, you may feel most settled when something is happening. Short deadlines, friendly competition and physical making can lift your mood, while long waits or vague feedback may leave you irritable. Build bursts into your week: timed sketch sessions, quick prototypes, a brisk walk before a crit. Letting off steam gently helps you stay kind to yourself." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在白羊座，你给人的第一印象充满活力、直截了当。在面试或小组 crit 中，你可能很早就开口，作品集也常以一张大胆直接的图开场。这份自信让人记住你。也可以为别人和细微之处留些空间来平衡它：说完第一点后停一停，反问对方一个问题，让一件安静的作品紧跟在响亮的那件之后。",
        en: "With Aries rising, your first impression is energetic and forthright. In an interview or a group crit you may speak up early, and your portfolio can open with a bold, direct image. That confidence is memorable. Balance it by leaving room for others and for nuance: pause after your first point, ask a question back, and let a quieter piece follow the loud one." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在金牛座，你的创作身份扎根于感官。你可能会用“拿在手里的感觉”来判断一个想法，比起十件快速完成的东西，你常常更想要一件美好、做工扎实的物件。耐心就是你的方法。只是记得，慢并不等于固定：每个学期试一种新材料，让舒适持续转化为新的发现。",
        en: "With the Sun in Taurus, your creative identity is rooted in the senses. You may judge an idea by how it feels in the hand, and you often prefer one beautiful, well-made object to ten quick ones. Patience is your method. Just remember that slow need not mean fixed: try one new material each term, so comfort keeps turning into discovery." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在金牛座，你的情绪安稳取决于舒适和连续。可以预期的日常、一个属于你的角落、信得过的材料，能让你平静到敢于冒创作的险。计划突然改变，可能比你表现出来的更让你不安。事情变动时，回到身体的感觉：整理一下工作台，准备一份点心，用双手做一件小东西。",
        en: "With the Moon in Taurus, your emotional ease depends on comfort and continuity. A predictable routine, a corner that is yours and materials you trust can make you calm enough to take creative risks. Sudden changes of plan may unsettle you more than you let on. When things shift, return to the body: tidy your bench, make a snack, make something small with your hands." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在金牛座，你给人沉着、踏实、可以信赖的感觉。导师可能会先注意到你作品完成度里的用心，以及作品集的整体连贯。这份稳定让人安心。为了同时展现你的广度，可以放进一件冒过险或转过方向的作品，并准备好谈一谈：当事情没有按计划走时，你学到了什么。",
        en: "With Taurus rising, you come across as calm, grounded and trustworthy. Tutors may notice the care in your finishes and the coherence of your portfolio before anything else. That steadiness reassures people. To show your range as well, include one piece that took a risk or changed direction, and be ready to talk about what you learned when it did not go to plan." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在双子座，你的创作身份存在于连接与多样之中。当想法彼此碰撞时，你可能状态最好：文字与图像、一场对话与一种材料、两个毫不相干的参考同时出现在一件作品里。同时处理多件事，本身就是你思考的方式。为了给它形状，可以维护一份“总枢纽”文档，让所有线索在那里汇合，每周回看一次，看看哪些已经可以编织在一起。",
        en: "With the Sun in Gemini, your creative identity lives in connection and variety. You may be at your best when ideas collide: text with image, a conversation with a material, two unrelated references in one piece. Juggling is part of how you think. To give it shape, keep a single “hub” document where all your threads meet, and revisit it weekly to see which ones are ready to weave together." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在双子座，你常常通过说出来、写下来消化情绪。和同伴聊几句、录一段语音，或写满一页笔记，就能把混乱的一天变成一个想法。沉默与孤立可能让你坐立不安。在工作时间里安排一些轻松的交流，随身带一本小笔记本，让你的头脑总有地方安放那些火花。",
        en: "With the Moon in Gemini, you often process feelings by talking and writing them through. A quick chat with a peer, a voice note or a page of notes can turn a confusing day into an idea. Silence and isolation may make you restless. Build light social contact into studio time, and keep a pocket notebook, so your mind always has somewhere to put its sparks." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在双子座，你给人活泼、好奇、能言善道的印象。面试时你可能回答得很快、涉猎很广，这很吸引人。也要让线索容易被跟上：准备一句清楚的话，说明你的项目之间靠什么相连，再用速写本展示你的众多兴趣如何彼此滋养。当逻辑清晰可见时，多样就会被读作优势。",
        en: "With Gemini rising, you come across as lively, curious and articulate. In interviews you may answer quickly and range widely, which can be engaging. Make sure the thread is easy to follow: prepare one clear sentence about what links your projects, and let a sketchbook show how your many interests feed one another. Variety reads as strength when its logic is visible." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在巨蟹座，你的创作身份与关怀、记忆和归属感紧紧相连。家庭照片、传下来的物件、童年的地方，或者“庇护”这个概念，都可能贯穿你的作品。这份情感上的真诚，能深深打动人。也要学会选择分享什么来保护它：借助隐喻、材料或距离，你可以做非常个人的作品，同时不必让自己毫无防备。",
        en: "With the Sun in Cancer, your creative identity is bound up with care, memory and belonging. Family photographs, inherited objects, childhood places or the idea of shelter may run through your work. That emotional honesty can move people deeply. Protect it by choosing what you share: through metaphor, material or distance, you can make very personal work without leaving yourself unguarded." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在巨蟹座，你的情绪节奏可能有起有落，周围的环境对你影响很大。温暖熟悉的空间、了解你的人，能帮助你打开自己、大胆实验。一次严厉的 crit 可能会在心里停留很久。为反馈日安排一个小小的照顾自己的流程：一顿喜欢的饭、一通打回家的电话，或者一小时只为自己而做的创作。",
        en: "With the Moon in Cancer, your emotional rhythm may ebb and flow, and your surroundings matter a great deal. A warm, familiar space and people who know you well can help you open up and experiment. A harsh crit may linger. Plan a small aftercare routine for feedback days: a favourite meal, a call home or an hour of making just for yourself." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在巨蟹座，你给人温和、体贴、容易亲近的感觉。面试官可能在听到你的想法之前，就先感受到你的温度。你起初也许显得有点害羞，等到空间让你感到安全后才慢慢打开。可以主动促成这一点：早一点到场，从你最有感情的那件作品讲起，并记得：真诚本身就是很有力的第一印象。",
        en: "With Cancer rising, you come across as gentle, attentive and easy to talk to. Interviewers may sense warmth before they hear your ideas. You might seem shy at first, then open up once the room feels safe. Help that happen: arrive a little early, start with the piece you feel closest to, and remember that sincerity is itself a strong first impression." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在狮子座，你的创作身份通过自我表达与慷慨而闪耀。你可能希望作品被看见、被感受、被记住，也常常在呈现上倾注真心。这份自信能鼓舞整个工作室。为了让它扎根，也做一些纯粹为自己、不给人看的作品，让你的价值感既来自掌声，也来自创作本身。",
        en: "With the Sun in Leo, your creative identity shines through self-expression and generosity. You may want your work to be seen, felt and remembered, and you often put real heart into presentation. That confidence can inspire a whole studio. Keep it rooted by making some work purely for yourself, unseen, so your sense of worth comes from the making as well as the applause." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在狮子座，当你的努力被注意到、被温暖地接纳时，你可能最像自己。鼓励给你力量；冷漠有时比批评更让你沉重。去寻找那些既坦诚又懂得欣赏的反馈环境，也练习给自己肯定：记下那些小小的成就，在开始评判一件作品之前，先庆祝自己完成了它。",
        en: "With the Moon in Leo, you may feel most yourself when your efforts are noticed and warmly received. Encouragement fuels you; indifference can feel heavier than criticism. Seek out spaces with honest, appreciative feedback, and practise giving yourself credit too: keep a list of small wins, and celebrate finishing a piece before you start judging it." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在狮子座，你给人温暖、自信、富有表现力的感觉。你在房间里的存在感常常很强，精心编排的作品集也很适合你。不过要让作品走在前面：给每一件作品说话的空间，既讲你的愿景，也讲你的过程，并展示成品背后的草稿。坦然谈起挣扎，会让你的光芒更有说服力。",
        en: "With Leo rising, you come across as warm, confident and expressive. Your presence in a room is often noticed, and a well-staged portfolio suits you. Let the work lead, though: give each piece room to speak, explain your process as well as your vision, and show the drafts behind the finished image. Being open about struggle makes your radiance more convincing." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在处女座，你的创作身份通过打磨与“好用”建立起来。你可能热爱测试、调整、改进，直到一件作品真正成立：也许是更干净的版式，也许是更牢靠的接口。对你来说，手艺就是一种关怀。提前设定一个“够好了”的节点来保持全局感；越过它之后，打磨可能会悄悄取代创造新的东西。",
        en: "With the Sun in Virgo, your creative identity is built through refinement and usefulness. You may love testing, adjusting and improving until a piece truly works, whether that means a cleaner layout or a sturdier joint. Craft is a form of care for you. Keep perspective by setting a “good enough” point in advance; beyond it, refining can quietly replace making something new." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在处女座，你可能在秩序和小而可完成的任务中找到平静。情绪起伏时，整理材料或规划这一周就能让你安定下来。你也可能是对自己最严厉的批评者。试着每天结束时记下三件进展顺利的事，并提醒自己：粗糙的草稿是方法的一部分，而不是对你的否定。",
        en: "With the Moon in Virgo, you may find calm in order and in small, achievable tasks. When feelings run high, sorting your materials or planning the week can steady you. You might also be your own harshest critic. Try ending each day by noting three things that went well, and remind yourself that rough drafts are part of the method, not evidence against you." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在处女座，你给人周到、谦逊、条理分明的印象。面试官可能马上就信任你对细节的关注。不过你也许会低估自己，先说缺点再说优点。提前准备好在每个项目里说出你引以为傲的地方，并放进一页不那么精致、更具实验性的内容，让人看见你精确背后的好奇心。",
        en: "With Virgo rising, you come across as thoughtful, modest and well organised. Interviewers may trust your attention to detail straight away. You might undersell yourself, though, by mentioning flaws before strengths. Prepare to name what you are proud of in each project, and include one less polished, more experimental page to show the curiosity behind your precision." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在天秤座，你的创作身份从平衡、美感与对话中生长出来。你可能会反复权衡每一个构图，直到它显得圆满；你也常常在双人或小组合作里如鱼得水，能感觉到每一种声音带来了什么。和谐是你的本能。也允许一些张力进来：一个偏离中心的裁切、一个悬而未决的问题，能让你的优雅多一分抓人的锋芒。",
        en: "With the Sun in Libra, your creative identity grows from balance, beauty and dialogue. You may weigh every composition until it feels resolved, and you often work well in pairs or groups, sensing what each voice adds. Harmony is your instinct. Let some tension in, too: an off-centre crop or an unresolved question can give your elegance an edge that holds attention." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在天秤座，你的情绪常常和周围的气氛连在一起。小组里的矛盾，或者凌乱、不美观的空间，可能让你心神不宁；而好的同伴能很快让你恢复过来。当决定显得无法回头时，你可能会犹豫。给自己一些小小的“做决定仪式”，比如午饭前在两个选项里选一个，让平衡不至于变成停滞。",
        en: "With the Moon in Libra, your mood is often tied to the atmosphere around you. Conflict in a group or a messy, unattractive space can unsettle you, while good company restores you quickly. You may hesitate when decisions feel final. Give yourself small deciding rituals, such as choosing between two options before lunch, so balance never turns into standstill." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在天秤座，你给人有礼、从容、富有审美感的印象。你的作品集可能编排得当，翻阅起来很舒服。在交谈中，你也许为了气氛顺畅而太快地附和。练习就自己的作品说出一个你坚定持有的观点；清晰的立场，与你天生的圆融放在一起，会非常好看。",
        en: "With Libra rising, you come across as courteous, composed and aesthetically aware. Your portfolio may feel well curated and pleasant to move through. In conversation, you might agree too readily to keep things smooth. Practise stating one opinion you hold firmly about your work; a clear point of view sits beautifully alongside your natural diplomacy." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在天蝎座，你的创作身份来自强度与蜕变。你可能一次又一次回到同一个题材，深挖下去，直到它显露出某种赤裸而真实的东西。浅尝辄止很难让你满足。这份深度很有力量。用一些更轻松的小项目或带玩心的练习来调节节奏，别让作品主题的分量，变成日子本身的分量。",
        en: "With the Sun in Scorpio, your creative identity draws on intensity and transformation. You may return again and again to one subject, digging until it reveals something raw and true. Half measures rarely satisfy you. That depth is powerful. Pace it with lighter side projects or playful exercises, so the weight of your themes doesn’t become the weight of your days." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在天蝎座，你的感受可能很强烈，却习惯藏在心里。你常常要对一个空间和其中的人足够了解，才能放松下来；反馈是否真诚，你也一眼就能分辨。为你最私人的作品挑选几位信任的读者，并给自己一个出口，比如一本私人速写本，里面的东西都不必拿出来示人。",
        en: "With the Moon in Scorpio, you may feel things powerfully yet keep them private. You often need to know a space and its people well before you relax, and you can tell when feedback is insincere. Choose a few trusted readers for your most personal work, and give yourself an outlet, such as a private sketchbook, where nothing needs to be shown." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在天蝎座，你给人专注、洞察力强、带点神秘感的印象。你还没说几句，别人可能就感觉到你的深度。在面试中，这种安静的强度很有吸引力；只要确保它显得开放，而不是防备。说出一个项目背后的个人原因，讲述时把目光放在作品上，也让你的好奇心流露出来。",
        en: "With Scorpio rising, you come across as focused, perceptive and a little enigmatic. People may sense depth before you say much. In interviews that quiet intensity can be compelling; just make sure it reads as open rather than guarded. Offer one personal reason behind a project, keep your eyes on the work as you speak, and let your curiosity show." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在射手座，你的创作身份在探索与大问题中成长。你可能会把旅行、哲学和其他文化连在一起，做出开阔而慷慨的作品。乐观让你一直向前。为了让这些想法落地，每个项目只选一个问题，并用具体的形式去检验它；冒险带着证据回到家时，才变成了艺术。",
        en: "With the Sun in Sagittarius, your creative identity grows through exploration and big questions. You may connect travel, philosophy and other cultures in work that feels expansive and generous. Optimism keeps you moving. To land those ideas, choose one question per project and test it in a concrete form; adventure becomes art when it comes home with evidence." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在射手座，空间、移动和新的视野会让你心情振奋。换个环境、去一趟美术馆，或者一份开放式的命题，都能让你恢复元气；僵硬的时间表则可能让你感到被困住。在结构里留出自由：每周给自己一个用来探索的下午，并把截止日期看成目的地，而不是篱笆。",
        en: "With the Moon in Sagittarius, your spirits lift with space, movement and new horizons. A change of scene, a gallery visit or an open-ended brief can restore you, while rigid timetables may feel confining. Build freedom into structure: give yourself one exploratory afternoon a week, and treat deadlines as destinations rather than fences." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在射手座，你给人热情、坦率、随和的印象。你的活力能让面试像一场聊天。不过你可能会说着说着就跑题，所以提前准备一条穿过作品集的简短路线，跑远时就回到它。你的真诚和幽默是加分项；再配上一两个精确的例子，就更有说服力。",
        en: "With Sagittarius rising, you come across as enthusiastic, frank and easy-going. Your energy can make an interview feel like a conversation. You may wander off on tangents, though, so prepare a short route through your portfolio and return to it when you drift. Your honesty and humour are assets; pair them with one or two precise examples." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在摩羯座，你的创作身份由抱负、自律和日积月累的手艺塑造。你可能会像盖房子一样对待作品集：先打地基，再往上加高。坚持是你安静的优势。记得给玩耍留出空间，因为那些看似“没产出”的实验，往往会成为你后来最有力作品的基石。",
        en: "With the Sun in Capricorn, your creative identity is shaped by ambition, discipline and craft built over time. You may treat a portfolio like a building, laying foundations before adding height. Persistence is your quiet advantage. Remember to leave room for play, because experiments that seem unproductive often become the cornerstone of your strongest later work." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在摩羯座，当你能掌控日程、看到进展时，你可能最有安全感。清单、里程碑和早早开工能让你平静下来。你也可能用“能干”把压力藏起来。当某一周特别吃力时，告诉一位信任的同伴或导师，并把休息当作计划的一部分，而不是必须挣来的奖励。",
        en: "With the Moon in Capricorn, you may feel safest when you are in control of your schedule and can see progress. Lists, milestones and early starts can calm you. You might also hide stress under competence. Let a trusted peer or tutor know when a week is heavy, and count rest as part of the plan rather than a reward you must earn." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在摩羯座，你给人沉稳、认真、能干的印象。你还没开口，充分的准备可能就已经替你说话了。这份可靠让人安心；也让温度流露出来吧。分享一个项目让你兴奋的地方，而不只是你如何把它管理好；再放进一件能透出幽默或脆弱的作品。情感的深度，会让你的能干更让人记住。",
        en: "With Capricorn rising, you come across as composed, serious and capable. Your preparation may speak for you before you do. That reliability is reassuring; let warmth show too. Share what excites you about a project, not only how you managed it, and include one piece that reveals humour or vulnerability. Depth of feeling makes your competence memorable." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在水瓶座，你的创作身份与原创性、与比个人更宏大的想法连在一起。你可能会设计系统、质疑惯例，或者想象社群可以怎样不同地生活。独立思考是你的天赋。也要把“人的尺度”放在视野里：用一个真实的人或地方检验你的概念，让远见与同理心同行。",
        en: "With the Sun in Aquarius, your creative identity is linked to originality and to ideas bigger than yourself. You may design systems, question conventions or imagine how communities could live differently. Independent thought is your gift. Keep the human scale in view: test your concept on one real person or place, so that vision and empathy travel together." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在水瓶座，你可能习惯隔着一点距离来消化情绪，借助想法、友谊与理念。在集体中，你常常需要属于自己的空间，过多的情绪压力可能让你退开。欢迎差异的工作室很适合你。也要和自己确认一下：每周写一段自己真实的感受，让你的心也留在对话里。",
        en: "With the Moon in Aquarius, you may process feelings at a little distance, through ideas, friendships and causes. You often need personal space within a group, and too much emotional pressure can make you withdraw. Studios that welcome difference suit you. Check in with yourself, too: a weekly note on how you actually feel keeps your heart in the conversation." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在水瓶座，你给人独特、友好、又有点出人意料的印象。你的作品集可能以不寻常的形式或角度让人眼前一亮。这份新鲜感让人难忘。帮助别人跟上你的思路：说明每个项目回应的是什么问题，并把你的实验和日常经验连起来，让你的想法显得亲切，而不是遥远。",
        en: "With Aquarius rising, you come across as original, friendly and a little unexpected. Your portfolio may surprise people with an unusual format or angle. That freshness is memorable. Help others follow your thinking: explain the problem each project responds to, and connect your experiments to everyday experience, so your ideas feel inviting rather than distant." } },
    { sun: { zh: "太阳在双鱼座，你的创作身份源自想象、共情与边界的消融。你可能在不同媒介、梦境与情绪之间自如穿梭，做出富有氛围或灵性的作品。敏感是你的乐器。给它一点结构吧：一套规则、一种固定的媒介，或一段规律的工作时间，让灵感找到可以靠岸的地方。",
        en: "With the Sun in Pisces, your creative identity flows from imagination, empathy and the dissolving of boundaries. You may move easily between media, dreams and moods, making work that feels atmospheric or spiritual. Sensitivity is your instrument. Give it a little structure, a set of rules, a fixed medium or a regular studio hour, so inspiration has a shore to land on." },
      moon: { zh: "月亮在双鱼座，你可能像海绵一样吸收身边人和环境的情绪。平静温和的空间、音乐和独自出神的时间能让你恢复；严苛的环境则可能很快让你疲惫。留意哪些感受是你自己的，哪些是你沾染上的。散一小段步，或者自由地画上几分钟，能帮助你重新回到自己。",
        en: "With the Moon in Pisces, you may absorb the moods of people and places like a sponge. Calm, gentle spaces, music and time alone to drift can restore you, while harsh environments may wear you down quickly. Notice which feelings are yours and which you have picked up. A short walk or a few minutes of free drawing can help you reset." },
      rising: { zh: "上升在双鱼座，你给人温柔、富有想象力、心地开放的印象。人们可能会被你作品里的世界吸引进去。面试时，你也许很难用语言把想法说定。提前准备几句关于主题和过程的“锚点”表达，让图像替你说一部分话；你的真诚与氛围会承担其余的部分。",
        en: "With Pisces rising, you come across as gentle, imaginative and open-hearted. People may feel drawn into the world of your work. In interviews you might struggle to pin ideas down in words. Prepare a few anchor phrases about your themes and process, and let images do some of the talking; your sincerity and atmosphere will carry the rest." } }
  ];

  L.resilienceDeep = {
    steady: { zh: "沉稳型的韧性意味着，批评和不确定很少让你偏离方向。你往往能听进尖锐的反馈，挑出有用的部分，然后继续往前。在高强度的课程里，这份从容很宝贵。需要留意的是察觉自己的极限：定期和自己确认状态，因为沉稳的人有时会在早该停一停的时候，依然一直撑下去。",
      en: "A steady resilience profile suggests that critique and uncertainty rarely knock you off course. You can often hear hard feedback, sort out what is useful and carry on. That composure is valuable on intensive courses. The thing to watch is your own limits: check in with yourself regularly, because steady people sometimes keep going long after they needed a pause." },
    balanced: { zh: "平衡型的韧性意味着，反馈确实会影响你，但你通常能找回自己的节奏。一次艰难的 crit 之后，你可能会低落一阵，一两天后又带着新想法回来。这样的循环是健康的。用一套简单的恢复流程来支持它，比如记笔记、休息，再迈出一个小小的下一步，让每次挫折都变成素材，而不是停滞。",
      en: "A balanced resilience profile suggests that feedback does affect you, and that you usually find your way back. You may feel a dip after a tough crit, then return with fresh ideas a day or two later. That cycle is healthy. Support it with a simple recovery routine, such as notes, rest and one small next step, so each setback becomes material rather than a stall." },
    sensitive: { zh: "细腻型的韧性意味着，你对反馈和氛围的感受都很深。这份敏感常常让你的作品敏锐、情感丰富，也意味着环境对你很重要：具体而温和的反馈、更舒缓的节奏和信任的同伴，能帮助你更好地成长。参加 crit 前，先写下你自己想问的问题；听到意见后，也允许自己花些时间消化，再作回应。",
      en: "A sensitive resilience profile suggests that you feel feedback and atmosphere deeply. That sensitivity often makes your work perceptive and emotionally rich. It also means environment matters: specific, kind feedback, a calmer pace and trusted peers can help you flourish. Prepare for crits by writing down your own questions first, and allow yourself time to absorb comments before responding." }
  };

  // ---- email only ----

  // Index = element: as your useful element (用神), and as an unfavourable one (忌神).
  L.yongDeep = [
    { use: { zh: "木为你的用神时，以生长为导向的习惯可能对你有帮助。多用纸、木材、竹子、植物和天然纤维；去户外或靠近绿地的地方画速写；在属于木的早晨开始新项目。乐于分享想法、愿意带人的合作者能帮到你；那些可以一周周看着它生长的长期项目，同样适合你。",
        en: "When Wood is your useful element (用神), growth-oriented habits may support you. Work with paper, timber, bamboo, plants and natural fibres; sketch outdoors or near green space; start projects in the morning, Wood’s time of day. Collaborators who are generous with ideas and keen to mentor can help, as can long-term projects you can watch develop week by week." },
      avoid: { zh: "木为你的忌神时，过度扩张可能会分散你的精力。留意那些旧想法还没完成、新想法却不断冒芽的时刻，或者抱负跑在了能力前面的时候。用带有金性的习惯来约束木：果断删改，给研究设定边界，做完一件再种下一件。生长经过修剪，才最有力量。",
        en: "When Wood is unfavourable (忌神) for you, too much expansion may scatter your energy. Notice when new ideas keep sprouting before old ones are finished, or when ambition outruns your capacity. Keep Wood in check with Metal-like habits: edit firmly, set limits on research, and finish one thing before planting the next. Growth works best when it is pruned." } },
    { use: { zh: "火为你的用神时，温暖与被看见可能会为你注入能量。在明亮、有阳光的空间里工作；多用光、色彩和投影；常常展示作品，哪怕只是非正式的。热情、善于表达的合作者能带动你；中午时段的工作、开放工作室和夏季集训，也同样适合。让作品在还在发展的时候，就被人看见。",
        en: "When Fire is your useful element, warmth and visibility may energise you. Work in bright, sunlit spaces; use light, colour and projection; show your work often, even informally. Enthusiastic, expressive collaborators can lift you, and so can midday working sessions, open studios and summer intensives. Let your work be seen while it is still developing." },
      avoid: { zh: "火为你的忌神时，过多的强度可能会让你疲惫。留意深夜的爆发式赶工、过热的小组氛围，或者不断要“表现”的压力。用带有水性的习惯来平衡火：安静的研究、清凉平和的工作空间、规律的睡眠，以及留给反思的时间。火苗温和一些，才能烧得更久，也让你更清楚地看见自己的作品。",
        en: "When Fire is unfavourable, too much intensity may tire you. Watch for late-night bursts, overheated group dynamics or the pressure to perform all the time. Balance Fire with Water-like habits: quiet research, cool and calm workspaces, regular sleep and time for reflection. A gentler flame lasts longer and lets you see your work more clearly." } },
    { use: { zh: "土为你的用神时，让人落地的日常可能会让你安定。多用黏土、石膏、石材、陶瓷或厚纸；保持固定的每周作息和整洁的工作空间。可靠、耐心的合作者能帮到你，氛围平和安稳的工作室也同样适合。规律地回到同一个地方、同一套流程，让持续的积累建立起信心。",
        en: "When Earth is your useful element, grounding routines may steady you. Work with clay, plaster, stone, ceramics or heavy paper; keep a fixed weekly timetable and an orderly workspace. Reliable, patient collaborators can help you, as can studios with a calm, settled atmosphere. Return regularly to the same place and process, and let consistency build confidence." },
      avoid: { zh: "土为你的忌神时，过多的厚重可能会拖慢你。留意那些日常变成惰性的时候、计划过度而迟迟不开始的时候，或者把别人的担忧当成自己的担忧的时候。用带有木性的习惯来平衡土：快速速写、去新的地方、做短小的实验，冒一点健康的风险。时不时翻一翻土，土地才能保持肥沃。",
        en: "When Earth is unfavourable, too much heaviness may slow you down. Notice when routine becomes inertia, when you over-plan rather than begin, or when you carry other people’s worries as your own. Balance Earth with Wood-like habits: quick sketches, new places, short experiments and a little healthy risk. Keep the ground fertile by turning it over now and then." } },
    { use: { zh: "金为你的用神时，清晰和结构可能会让你更敏锐。多用金属、玻璃、摄影、字体排印或精确的数字工具；善用网格、清单和清晰的命题。直接坦诚的合作者能帮到你，像秋天那样专门用于删改与复盘的阶段，也同样适合。干净、明亮、不杂乱的空间，常常能让你的判断力发挥到最好。",
        en: "When Metal is your useful element, clarity and structure may sharpen you. Work with metal, glass, photography, typography or precise digital tools; use grids, checklists and clear briefs. Direct, honest collaborators can help you, and so can autumn-like phases of editing and review. Clean, bright, uncluttered spaces often let your judgement work at its best." },
      avoid: { zh: "金为你的忌神时，过多的严苛可能会束缚你。留意那些对自己过于苛刻、删改过度，或者规则多到没有玩耍余地的时候。用带有火性的温暖来柔化金：早一点分享作品，允许速写本里出错，选择给你鼓励的同伴。精确是需要时拿起的工具，而不是每时每刻都必须达到的标准。",
        en: "When Metal is unfavourable, too much rigour may cramp you. Watch for harsh self-criticism, over-editing or rules that leave no room to play. Soften Metal with Fire-like warmth: share work early, allow mistakes in a sketchbook, choose encouraging company. Precision is a tool you pick up when it helps, not a standard you must meet every hour." } },
    { use: { zh: "水为你的用神时，思考与流动可能会滋养你。广泛阅读，去图书馆和档案馆，在水边或安静的夜晚工作，试试水墨、水彩、影像或声音。善于思考、充满好奇的合作者能帮到你。在项目与项目之间留出空隙，让想法沉淀下来，因为静水最能映照清楚。",
        en: "When Water is your useful element, reflection and flow may nourish you. Read widely, visit libraries and archives, work near water or in quiet evening hours, and try ink, watercolour, moving image or sound. Thoughtful, curious collaborators can help you. Leave space between projects to let ideas settle, because still water reflects most clearly." },
      avoid: { zh: "水为你的忌神时，过多的漂流可能会让焦点变得模糊。留意那些研究没有尽头、担忧取代了动手，或者非要等到完美状态才肯开始的时候。用带有土性的习惯来收住水：固定的时段、可以触摸的材料、明确的交付物和固定的工作地点。给水流筑好河岸，它才能把你带到某个地方。",
        en: "When Water is unfavourable, too much drifting may blur your focus. Notice when research becomes endless, when worry replaces making, or when you wait for the perfect mood before starting. Contain Water with Earth-like habits: fixed hours, tangible materials, clear deliverables and a set place to work. Give the flow some banks and it can carry you somewhere." } }
  ];

  // Each chart pattern (格局) read as a creative temperament.
  L.gejuDeep = {
    "正财格": { zh: "正财格可以被读作一种“稳步耕耘”的创作气质。你可能看重可靠、做工扎实，并且真正对使用者有用的作品。你往往计划周到，善于管理材料与时间，也享受看着付出一点点变成一个完成的、看得见摸得着的结果。应用设计、为真实需求而做的插画、需要细致统筹的展览，都可能发挥你的长处。挑战在于冒险：比起尝试一个古怪的想法，你也许更愿意留在已知可行的路上。在每个项目里留出一小笔“实验预算”，几小时什么都不必见成效的时间，你也许会发现，务实不再是限制，而是起跳的跳板。",
      en: "A Direct Wealth pattern (正财格) can be read as a temperament of steady cultivation. Creatively, you may value work that is reliable, well made and genuinely useful to the people who meet it. You often plan carefully, manage materials and time well, and enjoy seeing effort turn into a finished, tangible result. Applied design, illustration for real needs and exhibitions that call for careful logistics can all play to your strengths. The challenge is risk. You might stay with what you know works rather than test an odd idea. Build a small “experiment budget” into each project, a few hours where nothing has to work out, and you may find your practicality becomes a launchpad rather than a limit." },
    "偏财格": { zh: "偏财格可以被读作一种善抓机会、视野宽广的创作气质。你可能很快就能看出一种材料的意外用法、一份命题里的空白，或者一次偶遇中可能长出的合作。你往往喜欢变化、喜欢人，也喜欢工作室之外的世界，能做出与广泛观众产生连接的作品。慷慨是你风格的一部分，你很乐意分享人脉与想法。需要留意的是同时铺开太多事情，把自己摊得太薄。每个学期选一个主项目，让其他的事情去滋养它。当你对可能性的直觉有一个可以回归的中心时，它才最有力量。",
      en: "An Indirect Wealth pattern (偏财格) can be read as a temperament of opportunity and range. You may be quick to spot an unexpected use for a material, a gap in a brief or a chance meeting that could grow into a collaboration. You often enjoy variety, people and the world beyond the studio, and you can make work that connects with wide audiences. Generosity is part of your style; you share contacts and ideas freely. The risk is spreading yourself thin across too many ventures at once. Choose one main project each term and let the others feed it. Your instinct for possibility is strongest when it has a centre to return to." },
    "食神格": { zh: "食神格可以被读作一种自在、慷慨的表达气质。你创作的样子，可能像有人为朋友下厨：带着享受、顺畅，以及天然的好品味。想法常常不费力地从你身上流出来，你的作品也可能显得轻松、幽默，或者让人会心一笑。你往往享受过程本身，这让你一直在做。需要留意的一面是安逸：你可能会回避更难的删改，或者让人不舒服的题材。时不时给自己一份需要严谨或带点不适的命题。把天然的自在与一个拉伸自己的挑战搭配在一起，往往会产出你最出人意料的作品。",
      en: "An Eating God pattern (食神格) can be read as a temperament of easy, generous expression. You may make work the way some people cook for friends: with pleasure, flow and a natural sense of taste. Ideas often come out of you without strain, and your pieces can feel relaxed, humorous or quietly delightful. You tend to enjoy the process for its own sake, which keeps you making. The shadow side is comfort: you may avoid the harder edit or the uncomfortable subject. Now and then, set yourself a brief that asks for rigour or a little discomfort. Pairing your natural ease with one stretching challenge often produces your most surprising work." },
    "伤官格": { zh: "伤官格可以被读作一种才华横溢、不服常规的气质。你可能会质疑命题里的每一条规则，而你最好的想法，常常恰恰来自做了“别人叫你别做”的事。你的作品可能锋利、机智、带有挑衅性，原创度很高，个人声音也很鲜明。这种格局中，语言、表演或评论方面的才能也很常见。挑战在于你的锋芒如何落地：一个大胆的想法，如果以争吵的方式出现，就可能被忽视。把规则学透，再有意识地打破它；讲清你的理由，也懂得选择值得争的事。只要被用心引导，你的不安分就会成为原创力真正的引擎。",
      en: "A Hurting Officer pattern (伤官格) can be read as a temperament of brilliance and rebellion. You may question every rule in a brief, and your best ideas often come from doing exactly what you were told not to. Your work can be sharp, witty, provocative and highly original, with a strong personal voice. A talent for language, performance or critique is common here. The challenge lies in how your edge lands: a bold idea can be dismissed if it arrives as a fight. Learn the rules well enough to break them deliberately, explain your reasoning, and pick your battles. Channelled with care, your restlessness becomes a real engine for originality." },
    "正官格": { zh: "正官格可以被读作一种正直、有秩序的气质。期待清晰时，你可能工作得最好；你也常常对公平、清晰和完成度给自己定下很高的标准。在小组项目里，你可能自然而然地让大家保持在正轨上，你的作品也往往显得深思熟虑、有担当、结构清晰。导师也许会把你当作可以信赖的榜样。需要留意的是谨慎：担心做得“不对”，可能会压低你自己的声音。允许自己在每个项目里有意地打破一条规则。结构是你的盟友，而当你亲自选择它的例外时，它才最有表现力。",
      en: "A Direct Officer pattern (正官格) can be read as a temperament of integrity and order. You may work best with clear expectations, and you often hold yourself to high standards of fairness, clarity and finish. In group projects you may naturally keep things on track, and your work can feel considered, responsible and well structured. Tutors may come to rely on you as an example. The risk is caution: worrying about doing it “correctly” can mute your own voice. Give yourself permission to break one rule per project, on purpose. Structure is your ally, and it becomes most expressive when you choose its exceptions yourself." },
    "七杀格": { zh: "七杀格可以被读作一种在压力下迎难而上的气质。你可能会被困难的命题、要求严格的导师和别人回避的题材吸引，而且往往在“分量很重”的时候做出最锋利的作品。你的创作可能带着紧迫感、韧劲和冒险的气息，让人一眼就能感受到。你很少惧怕辛苦。挑战在于，持续的压力会磨损任何人，而你对自己的要求可能比任何导师都更狠。在高强度阶段里安排好恢复的时间，也让一些挑战出于好奇，而不是为了证明什么。勇气里包含了休息，才能走得长远。",
      en: "A Seven Killings pattern (七杀格) can be read as a temperament of courage under pressure. You may be drawn to difficult briefs, demanding mentors and subjects others avoid, and you often do your sharpest work when the stakes feel high. Your practice can carry urgency, toughness and a sense of risk that people feel immediately. You are rarely afraid of hard work. The challenge is that constant pressure can wear anyone down, and you may push yourself harder than any tutor would. Build recovery into intense phases, and take on some challenges out of curiosity rather than to prove something. Courage lasts longest when it includes rest." },
    "正印格": { zh: "正印格可以被读作一种好学、善于滋养的气质。在动手之前，你可能热爱阅读、研究，想弄清楚想法从哪里来。你的作品常常有深度、有语境，也带着关怀；对需要讲解或鼓励的同伴来说，你也可能是一个慷慨的存在。导师与传统对你很重要。需要留意的是一直停留在“学习模式”里，积累了很多知识，却没有让它变成作品。给自己定一条规则：每读一小时，就动手做一小时。研究为你的创作提供根基，而动手，才让这些根伸向阳光。",
      en: "A Direct Resource pattern (正印格) can be read as a temperament of learning and nurture. You may love to read, research and understand where ideas come from before you make anything. Your work often carries depth, context and a sense of care, and you can be a generous presence for peers who need explanation or encouragement. Mentors and traditions matter to you. The risk is staying in study mode, gathering knowledge without letting it become work. Set yourself a rule: for every hour of reading, spend an hour making. Research gives your practice its roots, and making is how those roots reach towards the light." },
    "偏印格": { zh: "偏印格可以被读作一种独到洞察的气质。比起教科书，你可能更多是通过直觉、独处和奇特的联想来学习，也常常被那些被忽略的东西吸引：边缘的历史、奇怪的材料、非常规的方法。你的作品可能独一无二、难以归类，而这正是它力量的一部分。你也许比别人需要更多独处的时间。需要留意的是陷入孤立，或者兴趣一转移就放下项目。和一位信任的同伴分享进行中的作品，并保留一份未完成想法的清单，时不时回去看看。当你独特的角度一直与他人保持对话时，它会变得更有力量。",
      en: "An Indirect Resource pattern (偏印格) can be read as a temperament of unusual insight. You may learn through intuition, solitude and odd connections rather than textbooks, and you are often drawn to the overlooked: marginal histories, strange materials, unconventional methods. Your work can feel singular and hard to categorise, which is part of its power. You may need more time alone than others do. The risk is isolation, or dropping projects when your interest shifts. Share work in progress with one trusted peer, and keep a running list of half-finished ideas to revisit. Your unique angle grows stronger when it stays in conversation with others." },
    "建禄格": { zh: "建禄格可以被读作一种自立自强的气质。日主在月令中有自己的根，所以当你靠自己的力气、按自己的节奏把事情做起来时，你可能最有底气。你往往有耐力、有独立性，也清楚自己想做什么。亲自动手、自主推进的项目很适合你。挑战在于接受帮助：你可能会把所有事情都揽在自己身上，或者不太愿意听取建议。试着在每个项目的早期，就邀请一位合作者或导师参与进来。懂得分担的自立，会比独自硬扛更有力量，也能让你走得更从容。",
      en: "A Thriving Prosperity pattern (建禄格) can be read as a temperament of self-reliance. Your Day Master finds its own root in the month branch, so you may feel most capable when you build things through your own effort and at your own pace. You often have stamina, independence and a clear sense of what you want to make. Hands-on, self-directed projects can suit you well. The challenge is accepting help: you might take everything on alone or resist advice. Try inviting one collaborator or mentor into each project at an early stage. Self-reliance becomes even more powerful when it knows how to share the load." },
    "月刃格": { zh: "月刃格可以被读作一种冲劲十足的气质。日主在月令遇到很强的比劫之气，可能表现为决心、好胜心和全力投入的勇气。在创作上，你可能大胆落笔、迅速投入，硬是穿过别人会绕开的障碍。你的作品常常带着真实的力量。挑战在于收放：锋利的能量也可能变成对自己或他人的急躁。给这把刀配一个刀鞘吧：稳定的日常、清晰的命题，再加上运动或大尺度制作这样的身体出口。引导得当，你的强度就会化为精准。",
      en: "A Month Blade pattern (月刃格) can be read as a temperament of fierce drive. Your Day Master meets a very strong peer energy in the month branch, which can show up as determination, competitiveness and a willingness to go all in. Creatively, you may work boldly, commit quickly and push through obstacles others would walk around. Your work can carry real force. The challenge is control: sharp energy can turn into impatience with yourself or others. Give that blade a sheath, through a steady routine, a clear brief and a physical outlet such as sport or large-scale making. Directed well, your intensity becomes precision." },
    "月劫格": { zh: "月劫格可以被读作一种在同伴之间成形的气质。月令中带着很强的同类之气，所以一个同届群体、一位对手或一项共同的目标，都可能让你充满干劲。你往往在与他人并肩时学得最快：友好的竞争、交换技法，或者一起筹办一场群展。忠诚和直率是你常见的长处。需要留意的是比较：拿自己的作品去衡量同伴，可能会扰乱你自己的方向。把别人当作同行的伙伴，而不是一把尺子。私下记录自己的进步，你也许会发现，合作正是你最可靠的动力来源。",
      en: "A Month Rob Wealth pattern (月劫格) can be read as a temperament shaped by peers. The month carries a strong companion energy, so you may be energised by a cohort, a rival or a shared cause. You often learn fastest alongside others, through friendly competition, swapping techniques or putting on a group show together. Loyalty and directness are typical strengths. The risk is comparison: measuring your work against a peer’s can unsettle your own direction. Treat others as company, not as a yardstick. Keep a private record of your own progress, and you may find that collaboration becomes your most reliable source of momentum." }
  };

  // Index = Day Master element: materials, colours, rhythms, spaces and seasons that suit it.
  L.elementPractice = [
    { zh: "对木日主来说，与“生长”相呼应的材料和节奏，可能让创作更加茂盛。纸、木材、竹子、亚麻、植物染料和绘画工具，在你手里往往显得很自然。绿色、清新的蓝，以及新叶般的浅色调，可能很适合你的色板。早晨和春天带着木气上升的能量，所以不妨在一天的早些时候、一学期的开头启动新项目。有自然光、有植物、有向外视野的空间，能帮助你的想法分枝。用稳定的每日生长作为节奏：一天一页，一早一张速写。最重要的是，给项目足够的时间成熟，并定期修剪，让最强壮的枝芽得到阳光。",
      en: "For a Wood Day Master, your practice may flourish with materials and rhythms that echo growth. Paper, timber, bamboo, linen, plant dyes and drawing tools often feel natural in your hands. Greens, fresh blues and the pale tones of new leaves can suit your palette. Mornings and spring tend to carry Wood’s rising energy, so begin new projects early in the day and early in the term. Spaces with daylight, plants and a view outwards can help your ideas branch. Work in a rhythm of steady daily growth: a page a day, a sketch each morning. Above all, give your projects time to mature, and prune them regularly so the strongest shoots get the light." },
    { zh: "对火日主来说，带着光与热的材料和节奏，可能让创作鲜活起来。光本身、投影、霓虹、亮面颜料、摄影、表演和基于屏幕的作品，可能都让你觉得得心应手。红色、橙色、暖粉色和明亮饱和的点缀色，可能很适合你的色板，再用几笔冷色来平衡。正午与夏天常常是火气最旺的时候，所以把最需要被看见的工作安排在这时：陈述、拍摄、开放工作室。明亮、热闹、光线好的空间能提振你。用“爆发”加“有意冷却”的节奏工作，留出反思与休息的时间。当你自己决定何时熊熊燃烧、何时只是温暖发光时，你的火焰才最能持久。",
      en: "For a Fire Day Master, your practice may come alive with materials and rhythms that carry light and warmth. Light itself, projection, neon, glossy paint, photography, performance and screen-based work can all feel natural. Reds, oranges, warm pinks and bright, saturated accents may suit your palette, balanced by a few cooler notes. Midday and summer often hold Fire’s peak energy, so schedule your most visible work then: presentations, shoots, open studios. Bright, sociable spaces with good light can lift you. Work in bursts followed by deliberate cooling-off periods for reflection and rest. Your flame lasts longest when you choose when it blazes and when it simply glows." },
    { zh: "对土日主来说，让人踏实的材料和节奏，可能让创作沉稳而深厚。黏土、陶瓷、石膏、石材、矿物颜料、厚纸和织物，往往很合你的手。赭石、陶土红、沙色、暖灰和低饱和的棕色，可能很适合你的色板。土主四季之间的过渡，所以比起剧烈的爆发，你可能更适合稳定的循环，并设置规律的复盘节点。平和、有序、整理得当的空间，最好贴近地面、靠近材料，能帮助你专注。保持一致的每周节奏，和一套信得过的工具。让你的创作像土壤一样一层层累积，偶尔加入一点小小的扰动，让它保持肥沃。",
      en: "For an Earth Day Master, your practice may settle and deepen with materials and rhythms that feel grounded. Clay, ceramics, plaster, stone, earth pigments, heavy paper and textiles often suit your hands. Ochres, terracotta, sand, warm greys and muted browns may suit your palette. Earth governs the transitions between seasons, so you may work well in steady cycles with regular review points rather than dramatic bursts. Calm, orderly spaces, ideally at ground level and close to your materials, can help you focus. Keep a consistent weekly rhythm and a reliable set of tools. Let your practice accumulate like soil, layer by layer, and add small disruptions now and then to keep it fertile." },
    { zh: "对金日主来说，强调清晰与精确的材料和节奏，可能让创作更加锐利。金属、玻璃、摄影、版画、字体排印、数字工具和细线描绘，往往让你得心应手。白色、银色、冷灰和黑色，再加一抹干净的点缀色，可能很适合你的色板。秋天和傍晚常常带着金气收敛的能量，是删改、复盘和收尾的好时机。干净、明亮、不杂乱、工具称手的空间，能让你的判断力发挥到最好。用清晰的阶段推进工作：从制作，到筛选，再到打磨。偶尔允许自己凌乱地玩一玩，让精确保持生气，让严谨始终是一种选择，而不是一只笼子。",
      en: "For a Metal Day Master, your practice may sharpen with materials and rhythms that emphasise clarity and precision. Metal, glass, photography, printmaking, typography, digital tools and fine-line drawing often feel natural. Whites, silvers, cool greys and black, with one clean accent, may suit your palette. Autumn and late afternoon often carry Metal’s gathering energy, so they are good times for editing, reviewing and finishing. Clean, bright, uncluttered spaces with good tools can help your judgement work at its best. Work in clear phases, from making to selecting to refining. Allow occasional messy play to keep your precision lively, so that rigour stays a choice rather than a cage." },
    { zh: "对水日主来说，容许流动与沉思的材料和节奏，可能让创作更有深度。水墨、水彩、影像、声音、写作、档案和装置，往往让你觉得很自然。深蓝、黑色、墨灰和半透明的层叠，可能很适合你的色板。夜晚和冬天常常带着水气安静内敛的能量，适合研究、阅读，以及让想法慢慢孵化。安静的空间，靠近水边或光线柔和，能帮助你的思绪沉淀。用“收集”与“停顿”交替的节奏工作：广泛收集，再让想法静置一阵，然后才去塑造它。给流动一个清晰的容器，一个形式或一个截止日期，让你的深度浮出水面。",
      en: "For a Water Day Master, your practice may deepen with materials and rhythms that allow flow and reflection. Ink, watercolour, moving image, sound, writing, archives and installation often feel natural. Deep blues, blacks, inky greys and translucent layers may suit your palette. Evenings and winter often hold Water’s quiet, inward energy, so they can be good times for research, reading and incubating ideas. Quiet spaces, near water or with soft light, can help your thoughts settle. Work in a rhythm of gathering and pausing: collect widely, then let ideas rest before you shape them. Give the flow a clear container, a format or a deadline, so your depth reaches the surface." }
  ];

  // Aries … Pisces (Sun sign): typical creative block; how to get unstuck; a small studio ritual.
  L.zodiacEmail = [
    { block: { zh: "你典型的卡顿，可能出现在激动人心的开头之后，当项目需要的是耐心而不是冲劲时。中段会显得平淡，你也许很想丢下它去做新的东西。有时卡顿来自挫败感：导师让你放慢一点，而这份停顿让你觉得像被拽住了。",
        en: "Your typical block may arrive after the exciting start, when a project needs patience rather than energy. The middle stretch can feel flat, and you might be tempted to abandon it for something new. Sometimes the block is frustration: a tutor asks you to slow down, and the pause feels like being held back." },
      remedy: { zh: "把中段变成一连串短跑。把项目拆成一个个小挑战，每个都有自己的小截止期，并把每一个都当作新的开始。身体的运动也有帮助：换一种工具，站起来工作，或者大幅放大尺寸。动力一旦回来，你通常就会想起当初是什么让这个想法令你兴奋。",
        en: "Turn the middle into a series of short sprints. Break the project into small challenges with their own mini deadlines, and treat each one as a fresh start. Physical movement helps too: change your tools, stand up to work, or scale up dramatically. Once momentum returns, you will usually remember why the idea excited you." },
      ritual: { zh: "每次进入工作状态前，先做十分钟的“第一笔”热身：一张又快又无畏的画，不用橡皮，不做计划。写上日期，叠成一摞，从不评判。一个学期下来，这一摞就成了你能量的记录，也提醒你：开始，正是你的超能力。",
        en: "Begin each studio session with a ten-minute “first mark” warm-up: a fast, fearless drawing with no eraser and no plan. Date it, add it to a stack, and never judge it. Over a term, the stack becomes a record of your energy and a reminder that starting is your superpower." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能看起来像静止不动。当项目需要转向时，你也许会不停打磨同一个版本，而不愿冒险失去已经成立的部分。新工具、新软件或突然更改的命题，也可能让你产生抗拒，就像舒适和进步在往相反的方向拉扯。",
        en: "Your block may look like stillness. When a project needs a change of direction, you might keep refining the same version rather than risk losing what already works. New tools, new software or a sudden change of brief can also make you resistant, as though comfort and progress were pulling in opposite directions." },
      remedy: { zh: "让改变变得具体而微小。先保存现在的版本，再在旁边故意做一个粗糙的变体，这样什么都不会失去。花十分钟摆弄一种新材料，不设任何目标。往往双手一投入，抗拒就会松动，好奇心也随之回来。稳定的、带着感官的探索，能让你重新动起来。",
        en: "Make change tangible and small. Save the current version, then make a deliberately rough variation alongside it, so nothing is lost. Handle a new material for ten minutes without any goal. Often, once your hands are involved, your resistance softens and curiosity returns. Steady, sensory exploration gets you moving again." },
      ritual: { zh: "在桌上放一个小小的“本周材料”托盘：一块碎布、一块石头、一种新纸、一件拾得物。每天早上花几分钟，只是触摸和观察它，记下一种特质。久而久之，这个慢慢的感官习惯，会为你积累起一座质感资料库，持续滋养你的作品。",
        en: "Keep a small “material of the week” tray on your desk: a fabric scrap, a stone, a new paper, a found object. Spend a few minutes each morning simply touching and observing it, noting one quality. Over time, this slow sensory habit builds a library of textures that feeds your work." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿常常来自“开了太多标签页”。几个想法同时竞争时，你可能难以选定一个并投入进去；新鲜感一过，无聊也随之而来。有时你把一个想法说得太透了，还没开始动手，真正去做它的冲动就已经消失了。",
        en: "Your block often comes from too many open tabs. With several ideas competing, you may struggle to choose one and commit, and boredom can set in once the novelty fades. Sometimes you talk an idea through so thoroughly that the urge to actually make it disappears before you begin." },
      remedy: { zh: "给每个想法一次快速、低成本的测试：一页纸、一小时、一个原型。然后挑出最让你惊喜的那一个。在项目内部保留变化：切换媒介，或者让研究与制作交替进行。在想法成形之前少说一点；等到有东西可以展示时，再开始聊。",
        en: "Give each idea a quick, cheap test: one page, one hour, one prototype. Then pick the one that surprised you most. Keep variety inside the project by switching media or alternating research and making. Talk less about an idea before it exists; let the conversation start once there is something to show." },
      ritual: { zh: "每天结束时写下三行：一件你做了的东西、一个你收集到的想法、一个留给明天的问题。全部写在同一本笔记本里。只要两分钟，既满足你对文字的喜爱，也让你四散的线索有一个汇合的地方。每到周日，把这一周的记录从头翻一遍。",
        en: "End each day by writing three lines: one thing you made, one idea you collected, one question for tomorrow. Keep them all in a single notebook. It takes two minutes, satisfies your love of words and gives your scattered threads a place to meet. Reread the week’s pages on Sunday." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能是情绪上的，而不是技术上的。一次严厉的 crit 之后，或者身处陌生的空间时，你也许会缩回自己，几乎什么都做不出来。你也可能担心个人化的作品暴露太多，于是把对你最重要的素材藏了起来。",
        en: "Your block may be emotional rather than practical. After a harsh crit, or in an unfamiliar space, you might withdraw and find it hard to make anything at all. You may also worry that personal work reveals too much, and hold back the material that matters most to you." },
      remedy: { zh: "在创作作品之前，先营造“家”的感觉。带一件熟悉的物件到工作室，在信任的人身边工作，或者从一段让你感到安全的记忆开始。当个人素材让你觉得太暴露时，就借助隐喻或材料把它转译出来。温和的条件，往往能打开深层的作品。",
        en: "Create a sense of home before you create work. Bring a familiar object into the studio, work beside someone you trust, or start from a memory that feels safe. When personal material feels exposing, translate it through metaphor or material. Gentle conditions often unlock deep work." },
      ritual: { zh: "为每个项目准备一个小小的“记忆盒”：一张照片、一张票根、一张字条、一种气味，任何与作品相连的私人物件。每次开始工作前，打开它，与它相处一分钟。它会提醒你这件作品为什么重要，也让你的温柔一直在身边。",
        en: "Keep a small “memory box” for each project: a photograph, a ticket, a note, a scent, anything personal that connects to the work. Before you begin a session, open it and spend a minute with it. It reminds you why the work matters and keeps your tenderness close." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能出现在没人看着的时候。长时间独自工作、没有反馈，会消耗你的动力；害怕做出平庸的东西，也可能让你迟迟无法开始。当项目需要谦逊时，比如临摹、练基本功或者推倒重来，你也可能感到卡住。",
        en: "Your block may appear when no one is watching. Long, solitary stretches without feedback can drain your motivation, and the fear of making something ordinary can stop you from beginning. You may also feel stuck when a project calls for humility, such as copying, practising basics or starting again." },
      remedy: { zh: "为自己创造一小群友善的观众。每周和一位同伴分享进展，或者在工作室的墙上办一场迷你展。提醒自己：带着玩心、不完美的作品，依然可以很慷慨。把练习看作一场你享受的演出前的排练，而不是一场可能失败的考试。",
        en: "Create a small, friendly audience. Share progress with one peer each week, or set up a mini show on your studio wall. Remind yourself that playful, imperfect work can still be generous. Treat practice as rehearsal for a performance you enjoy rather than a test you might fail." },
      ritual: { zh: "每周一次，把你做的所有东西都钉上墙，给它起个标题，就像策划一场小展览。花五分钟从它前面走过，留意那些让你骄傲的地方。这既是对付出的致意，也满足你的仪式感，并让自信始终与真实的作品连在一起。",
        en: "Once a week, pin up everything you have made and give it a title, as if curating a small exhibition. Spend five minutes walking past it, noticing what you are proud of. It honours your effort, satisfies your sense of occasion and keeps your confidence tied to real work." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿常常看起来像完美主义。一个细节早就成立了，你可能还在不停修改；或者非要把每一步都规划好，才肯开始。有时你把缺点看得太清楚，以至于整件作品都显得不对，哪怕别人觉得它已经快完成了。",
        en: "Your block often looks like perfectionism. You may keep correcting a detail long after it works, or delay starting until you have planned every step. Sometimes you see the flaws so clearly that the whole piece feels wrong, even when others think it is close to finished." },
      remedy: { zh: "把“做”和“评”分开。先定一个只管产出、不许修改的时段，再另留一段时间专门打磨。开始之前就定义好“完成”，比如三条标准，达到了就停下。问问一位信任的同伴哪里是成立的；他们的视角，能让你松开批判的手。",
        en: "Separate making from judging. Set a timer for generating, with no corrections allowed, then a separate slot for refining. Define “finished” before you start, perhaps with three criteria, and stop once they are met. Ask a trusted peer what is working; their view can loosen your critical grip." },
      ritual: { zh: "在速写本里留一页“美丽的意外”。每当有东西出了错却看起来很有意思，比如一块污渍、一张印坏的纸、一道歪了的切口，就贴进去或拍下来。时常翻看这一页，会提醒你：不完美常常通向新的地方，错误本身也可以成为素材。",
        en: "Keep a “happy accidents” page in your sketchbook. Whenever something goes wrong but looks interesting, such as a smudge, a misprint or a crooked cut, paste it in or photograph it. Looking back over this page reminds you that imperfection often leads somewhere new, and that mistakes can be material." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能是犹豫不决。面前有两三个不错的选项时，你也许会无休止地权衡，生怕选错。小组项目里的紧张气氛，或者不同导师意见相左，也可能让你僵住，因为你想同时让所有人满意，反而看不清自己真正的想法。",
        en: "Your block may be indecision. With two or three good options in front of you, you might weigh them endlessly, worried about choosing wrongly. Tension in a group project or conflicting opinions from different tutors can also freeze you, as you try to please everyone at once and lose sight of your own view." },
      remedy: { zh: "把决定变小，并且可以撤回。先选一个方案做一周，知道自己随时能换回来。在征求反馈之前，先写下你自己的想法，而不是别人的期待。有时候，最平衡的选择就是先投入，等作品出来了再调整。往往是动起来之后，答案才会自己浮现。",
        en: "Make decisions smaller and reversible. Choose one option for a week, knowing you can switch back. Write down what you think, not what others expect, before you ask for feedback. Sometimes the most balanced choice is to commit, then adjust once the work exists. Momentum often reveals the answer." },
      ritual: { zh: "每次开始工作前，用心布置你的工作空间：清理桌面，摆出两件工具，在视线里放一件好看的物件。这个小小的“构图”动作能让你平静下来，也让工作室变成一个你对和谐的眼光已经开始运作的地方，还没落笔，状态就已经到位了。",
        en: "Before each session, arrange your workspace with care: clear the surface, set out two tools, place one beautiful object in view. This small act of composition calms you and turns the studio into a space where your eye for harmony is already at work before the first mark is made." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能来自强度本身。当一个题材显得太私人或太沉重时，你也许会一直绕着它打转，却始终不碰它；或者偷偷地做，谁也不给看。有时一个项目还没来得及成长，你就因为觉得它“不够深”而彻底放弃了。",
        en: "Your block may come from intensity itself. When a subject feels too personal or too heavy, you might circle it without touching it, or work in secret and refuse to show anyone. Sometimes you abandon a project entirely because it doesn’t feel deep enough, rather than letting it grow." },
      remedy: { zh: "从侧面去接近核心题材。先通过一种相关的材料、一个象征，或一件更轻松的配套作品入手。挑一位信任的人，早一点看到作品。提醒自己：深度可以在一版又一版中慢慢累积，第一稿不必承载所有。让每一版都比上一版多揭开一点点。",
        en: "Approach the core subject indirectly. Work through a related material, a symbol or a lighter companion piece first. Choose one trusted person to see the work early. Remind yourself that depth can build up over versions; the first draft doesn’t need to carry everything. Let each version reveal a little more." },
      ritual: { zh: "准备一本谁都不会看到的私人日记，每次动手前写五分钟。让它承载那些赤裸的情绪和秘密，作品本身就可以被用心地塑造。这道边界保护着你的强度，也让它得以转化为艺术。什么从日记进入作品，由你自己决定。",
        en: "Keep a private journal that no one else will see, and write in it for five minutes before making. Let it hold the raw feelings and secrets, so the work itself can be shaped with care. This boundary protects your intensity and lets it become art. You choose what passes from the page into the work." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能来自被困住的感觉。细致的技术活、重复的制作或者范围很窄的命题，都可能让你坐立不安；项目还没真正完成，你也许就已经失去了兴趣。每当眼前的想法变难时，你还可能跳去追一个新的大想法。",
        en: "Your block may come from feeling confined. Detailed technical tasks, repetitive production or a narrow brief can make you restless, and you might lose interest before the project is fully realised. You may also leap to a new big idea whenever the current one becomes difficult." },
      remedy: { zh: "把困难的部分重新想象成一次探险。问问自己：完成它，会让你发现什么？再给自己定一个奖励，比如去看一场展，或者去一个新地方散步。换一天在别的地方工作。把细碎的任务和一个更大的问题连起来，能让你重新找到意义感。",
        en: "Reframe the hard part as an expedition. Ask what you will discover by finishing, and set yourself a reward, such as an exhibition visit or a walk somewhere new. Change your working location for a day. Connecting a detailed task to a bigger question can restore your sense of purpose." },
      ritual: { zh: "准备一本“田野笔记”，每次出门都带着，不论是长途旅行还是去楼下小店。记下一个画面、一句无意听到的话、一个问题。回到工作室后，挑一条笔记探索十分钟。小小的冒险，能让你的好奇心一直有燃料。",
        en: "Keep a “field notes” notebook for every outing, whether a long trip or a walk to the shop. Record one image, one overheard phrase and one question. Back in the studio, choose one note to explore for ten minutes. Small adventures keep your curiosity fuelled." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能来自你加在自己身上的压力。你也许觉得每件作品都必须对得起花掉的时间，于是实验显得像是放纵。当进展不明显时，你可能更用力地推，而不是退后一步；疲惫就这样悄悄取代了灵感。",
        en: "Your block may come from pressure you put on yourself. You might feel that every piece must justify the time spent on it, so experiments feel indulgent. When progress isn’t visible, you may push harder rather than step back, and tiredness can quietly replace inspiration." },
      remedy: { zh: "像安排工作一样认真地安排玩耍。每周给自己一个固定的、没有压力的时段，做那些不必成功的实验。把大目标拆成一个个小而看得见的里程碑。当你感到卡住时，先休息；你的坚持，会比你预想的更快回来，对创作的乐趣也一样。",
        en: "Schedule play as seriously as you schedule work. Give yourself a fixed, low-stakes slot each week for experiments that don’t need to succeed. Break big goals into small, visible milestones. And when you feel stuck, rest first; your persistence returns faster than you expect, and so does your pleasure in the work." },
      ritual: { zh: "每周五写一段简短的进度记录：完成了什么、学到了什么、下一步想尝试什么。再加上一件让你享受的小事。这个每周的习惯，能让你实实在在地看到自己的攀登，也提醒你：享受，本身就是构建长久之物的一部分。每个学期结束时，把这些记录从头读一遍。",
        en: "Every Friday, write a short progress log: what you completed, what you learned, what you will try next. Add one small thing you enjoyed. This weekly habit shows your climb in real terms and reminds you that enjoyment is part of building something lasting. Reread the logs at the end of each term." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能来自抽离感。一个想法作为概念时让你兴奋，可一旦要把它实实在在地做出来，吸引力就消退了。面对显得太常规的命题，你也许会抗拒；当一个小组朝着你并不认同的共识推进时，你也可能感到疏离。",
        en: "Your block may come from detachment. An idea can feel exciting as a concept but lose its pull once you have to make it physically. You might also resist a brief that feels conventional, or feel disconnected when a group pushes towards a consensus you don’t share." },
      remedy: { zh: "在限制之中找到实验。问问自己：怎样在回应一份常规命题的同时，也颠覆它？快速做一个粗糙的原型，把概念带到现实里来。和艺术圈外的人聊聊，比如一位工程师、一位邻居，能让你的想法重新与真实生活接上线。",
        en: "Find the experiment inside the constraint. Ask how you could subvert a conventional brief while still answering it. Build a quick, rough prototype to bring the concept into the world. Talking with someone outside art, an engineer or a neighbour, can reconnect your idea with everyday life." },
      ritual: { zh: "每周一次，给自己一份“不可能的命题”：用三十分钟设计一个荒诞的、未来的，或者故意行不通的东西。把结果放进一个文件夹。这样的玩耍让你的创造性思维保持自由，也为你积攒一批以后可以取用的奇特想法。其中有些，也许并没有看上去那么不可能。",
        en: "Once a week, set yourself an “impossible brief”: design something absurd, futuristic or deliberately unworkable in thirty minutes. Keep the results in a folder. This play keeps your inventive mind free and gives you a store of strange ideas to draw on later. Some may turn out to be less impossible than they looked." } },
    { block: { zh: "你的卡顿，可能像一场雾。想法以情绪或画面的形式到来，难以捕捉；当你试着把它们变得具体时，它们似乎就溜走了。你也可能把别人的情绪或批评吸收得太深，以至于自己的方向也变得模糊，不再确定自己原本想做的是什么。",
        en: "Your block may feel like fog. Ideas arrive as moods or images that are hard to pin down, and when you try to make them concrete, they seem to slip away. You may also absorb other people’s feelings, or their criticism, so strongly that your own direction blurs and you are no longer sure what you wanted to make." },
      remedy: { zh: "在塑造之前，先松松地把想法接住。录语音、收集图像、做快速的色彩小稿，让雾气慢慢沉淀成一组素材。给反馈设一道温和的边界：先记下来，离开一会儿，再决定留下什么。结构可以是柔软的，也依然能托住你。",
        en: "Catch ideas loosely before shaping them. Record voice notes, collect images, make quick colour studies, and let the fog settle into a collection. Set gentle boundaries around feedback: write it down, then step away before deciding what to keep. Structure can be soft and still hold you." },
      ritual: { zh: "每次开始工作时，先用五分钟听音乐、自由地画，让手不加评判地跟着声音走。如果有帮助，可以闭上眼睛画一部分。这个小小的仪式，能温和地打开你的想象，也标志着你从外部世界，转进了自己的内心世界。",
        en: "Begin each session with five minutes of music and free drawing, letting your hand follow the sound without judgement. Close your eyes for part of it if that helps. This small ritual gently opens your imagination and marks the shift from the outside world to your inner one." } }
  ];

  if (typeof module === "object" && module.exports) module.exports = L;
})(typeof window !== "undefined" ? window : globalThis);
